// =====================================================================
// 4. COMPILE-SAFETY REVIEW
// =====================================================================
//
// Pine Script v6 Constraint Checklist:
//
// ✓ No input.*() calls in any library
//     All libraries use only parameters; no input.int/float/string/bool.
//
// ✓ No request.security() calls in any library
//     All HTF/MTF data is fetched in main and passed via parameters.
//
// ✓ No nested UDT .new() in default field values
//     All UDT fields are primitive (string, color, bool, int, float).
//     No type fields reference other UDTs except arrays of simple UDTs.
//
// ✓ No function calls in type default fields
//     All UDT fields use Pine defaults (na for each type).
//
// ✓ var keyword in library functions
//     render_level() uses `var line l = na` and `var label lbl = na`.
//     Pine v6 scopes var per unique call site — each import site gets
//     its own persistent state. This is correct and safe.
//
// ✓ Export visibility
//     All library functions and UDTs use `export` keyword.
//     No non-exported helpers exist in libraries (all are generic).
//
// ✓ Import chain depth
//     tv_readiness_ui imports tv_dashboard_utils (depth 1).
//     tv_market_intel_ui imports tv_dashboard_utils (depth 1).
//     Main imports all 5 libraries directly.
//     Maximum depth = 2 (main → readiness_ui → dashboard_utils).
//     Pine v6 supports this without issues.
//
// ✓ Array parameters in library functions
//     render_market_intel() accepts AlertEntry[] and OBEntry[] arrays.
//     Pine v6 supports array<UDT> parameters in exported functions.
//
// ✓ Table reference passing
//     All render functions accept `table` as first parameter.
//     Pine v6 allows passing table references to library functions.
//
// ✓ Color parameter handling
//     All colors are passed as `color` type parameters.
//     No color constants are hardcoded in libraries except transparent
//     (#00000000) used for invisible label backgrounds in render_utils.
//
// ✓ Token budget estimate
//     Moved to libraries: ~800-900 lines of display/rendering code
//     - render_readiness_dashboard(): ~600 lines of table.cell() calls
//     - render_market_intel_dashboard(): ~90 lines
//     - render_level(): ~40 lines
//     - resolve_pos/format_*: ~15 lines
//     - Candidate preview box rendering: ~25 lines
//     Added to main: ~200 lines of DTO precomputation
//     Net savings in main indicator: ~600-700 lines / ~3000-3500 tokens

// =====================================================================
// 5. EDGE-PROTECTION NOTE
// =====================================================================
//
// The following proprietary components remain EXCLUSIVELY in the main
// invite-only indicator and are NEVER exposed through any library:
//
// SCORING & THRESHOLDS:
// - Confluence scoring weights and formulas
// - Mode-specific threshold tables (Aggressive/Balanced/Conservative)
// - Quality score computations (sweep, reclaim, MSS, displacement)
// - Activation gate thresholds and lifecycle minimums
//
// DETECTION ENGINES:
// - Liquidity sweep detection and grading (A/B/C)
// - Market Structure Shift (MSS) detection, body confirmation logic
// - Displacement detection (range/ATR ratio, body dominance, retention)
// - Reclaim detection (score accumulation, acceptance bar counting)
// - FVG detection and mitigation tracking
// - Order Block identification and confluence checking
//
// STATE MACHINES:
// - Lifecycle state machine (LC_IDLE → LC_ENTRY_ARMED progression)
// - Pending activation windows and delayed activation logic
// - Desync detection and invariant enforcement
// - Setup expiry, degradation, and conflict resolution
//
// STRATEGY LOGIC:
// - ICT 2022 eligibility gate computations
// - Silver Bullet session-based sweep requirements
// - Continuation context evaluation
// - Dominant-side resolution and score arbitration
//
// SIGNAL GENERATION:
// - Trade entry/exit signal creation
// - Webhook JSON payload construction
// - SL/TP computation and trade resolution
// - Performance tracking and equity curve
//
// What libraries CAN see:
// - Pre-rendered text strings (e.g., "Bullish shift confirmed (strong)")
// - Pre-resolved colors (e.g., uiPosColor for a passing condition)
// - Pre-formatted numbers (e.g., "85%" not the raw score)
// - Structural flags (e.g., hasPending=true, isTrader=false)
//
// Libraries CANNOT reverse-engineer:
// - What score threshold produced "High conviction"
// - What lifecycle stage maps to which action text
// - How mode qualification is determined
// - What conditions gate activation vs pending
// - Any detection algorithm parameters or weights
//
// The Display DTO pattern ensures a strict one-way data flow:
//   Main (proprietary logic) → DTO (opaque display values) → Library (generic rendering)
// This architectural boundary is enforced by the fact that DTOs contain
// ONLY rendered output (strings, colors, booleans) — never raw scores,
// thresholds, or intermediate computation values.

